# pots v1.4 — NEGATIVE. A symptom event whose symptom is the empty string.
#
# WHY THE EMPTY STRING AND NOT A MISSING PROPERTY. Dropping pots:symptom
# altogether would also be rejected, by sh:minCount 1 in the same constraint --
# but that is the easy case, and a producer is far more likely to write an empty
# value than to omit the field. A symptom picker with nothing selected, a text
# box the user tapped past, a converter defaulting an absent enum to "": each
# emits a symptom event whose symptom is present, correctly typed, and empty.
#
# sh:minLength 1 is what separates those two failures, and it is the half a shape
# author is most likely to leave out. This fixture is what proves it is there.
#
# EXACTLY ONE CONSTRAINT FIRES:
#   pots:SymptomEventShape / path pots:symptom / MinLengthConstraintComponent
#
# sh:datatype xsd:string is satisfied -- "" is a perfectly good string -- and so
# are sh:minCount and sh:maxCount, because the property IS present exactly once.
# Only the length constraint separates this record from a valid one, which is the
# claim the fixture exists to make.
#
# NOTE FOR ANYONE WRITING A SIBLING: sh:minLength counts characters after string
# conversion with no trimming, as SHACL defines it, so a symptom of " " (one
# space) has length 1 and PASSES. That is a real gap and it is not this
# vocabulary's alone -- jayostis/spec#26 and jayostis/spec#33 track it across all
# 28 required fields in the specification. Do not write a whitespace fixture here
# expecting it to fail; it will report NO_VIOLATION.
#
# Everything else is identical to symptomevent-lightheadedness.VALID.ttl apart
# from the subject IRI and the symptom value.
#
# Fully synthetic.

@prefix pots: <https://ns.cascadeprotocol.org/pots/v1#> .
@prefix xsd: <http://www.w3.org/2001/XMLSchema#> .

<urn:uuid:p0t5-0007-4a00-8000-00000000sy20> a pots:SymptomEvent ;
    pots:symptom "" ;
    pots:notes "Onset at four minutes upright; resolved on sitting." ;
    pots:timestampSeconds "240.0"^^xsd:double .
